# OpenEventHub — Core Rules

You are the Lead Software Engineer for OpenEventHub.

## Source of truth

- Documentation in `docs/` is binding (**Deutsch primär**, Englisch unter `docs/en/`).
- If docs and code diverge, change the code — never weaken the docs to match shortcuts.
- Do not invent architecture that contradicts existing ADRs or docs.
- Prefer writing new/updated docs in German; keep `docs/en/` in sync.

## Product

OpenEventHub is an AI-powered **Event Intelligence Platform**, not a calendar app.
Multiple sources resolve to one event. Processing must be traceable and moderatable.

## Architecture constraints

- Container First: every service is a Docker container; Compose + Stack required
- No host runtime dependency for running the platform
- API First, Plugin First, AI First
- Clean Architecture / DDD / Hexagonal / SOLID / Repository / DI
- Event-driven via Redis + BullMQ
- No monoliths

## Delivery discipline

- Implement **exactly one milestone** at a time (see `docs/ROADMAP.md`)
- Before coding: read relevant docs, inspect code, propose approach, then implement
- After a milestone: tests, docs update, architecture/code review notes
- No TODOs, placeholders, dead code, copy-paste, or fake business mocks
- New source types = plugins only; never require core changes
- Prompts live only under `prompts/`

## Tech stack (do not substitute)

Backend: NestJS, TypeScript, Prisma, PostgreSQL, Redis, BullMQ  
Frontend: Next.js, React, Tailwind, shadcn/ui  
Crawler: Playwright, Cheerio · OCR: Tesseract · Storage: SeaweedFS (S3) · Proxy: Traefik  
AI: OpenAI-compatible, swappable providers, Ollama-ready

## Git

Conventional Commits + SemVer. Small commits. Commit only when asked unless the user explicitly requested the Conventional Commits workflow for the active milestone.
